+# Design
+
+## Context
+
+See proposal.md and the product-stock-lifecycle spec. Product quantity is stored on the product/inventory model and cart/order flows currently validate or mutate it inconsistently. The two checkout endpoints share order processing, while cart mutations and private order status changes have separate API entry points.
+
+## Goals / Non-Goals
+
+**Goals:**
+
+- Centralize stock checks and quantity mutations around the product identified by each cart/order line.
+- Make multi-line operations validate before writing and execute in one transaction.
+- Use a conflict exception mapped to HTTP 409 at the REST boundary.
+- Make read-model availability a pure function of merchant availability and positive stock.
+
+**Non-Goals:**
+
+- Redesign inventory by region, variants, reservations, or warehouse allocation.
+- Change merchant-facing inventory editing APIs beyond keeping their quantity authoritative.
+
+## Decisions
+
+- Use the product referenced by each cart/order line as the mutation key, not list position or a separately queried product. This prevents one product's quantity being applied to another.
+- Validate all lines before decrementing and perform validation plus mutation in the existing service transaction. This provides all-or-nothing behavior for checkout and multi-update without introducing a new reservation table.
+- Treat cancellation as an idempotent state transition: only a non-canceled to canceled transition restores stock; canceled-to-other is a conflict. This avoids duplicate restoration.
+- Keep merchant `available` as an upper-level opt-out and compute effective availability from `available && quantity > 0`. This preserves merchant intent while hiding zero-stock products from purchase.
+- Add focused service/API tests around each endpoint contract, with model-level tests for stock calculations where existing test infrastructure permits.
+
+## Risks / Trade-offs
+
+- [Concurrent checkouts can race if stock rows are not locked] → use the product/inventory persistence transaction and an atomic conditional decrement or pessimistic lock where the repository supports it; recheck before commit.
+- [Existing exception mappings may expose stock errors as 5xx] → introduce/use a dedicated conflict exception and map it explicitly at both cart and order API boundaries.
+- [Legacy products may have inconsistent availability records] → use the same quantity source already returned by the v2 product representation and retain merchant availability as a separate flag.
+
+## Migration Plan
+
+Deploy application changes without a data migration; existing product quantities remain authoritative. Verify checkout, cart conflicts, cancellation idempotence, and v2 reads against representative products before enabling traffic.
